---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { faqs } from '../config/faqs';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const description =
	'Level 2 EV charger installation for Colorado homes and appropriate light-commercial properties, planned around panel capacity, the circuit, and how you drive.';
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="EV Charger Installation"
	description={description}
	eyebrow="Energy"
	heading="Charge at home. Start every day ready to drive."
	lede="Level 2 charging is substantially faster than a standard 120-volt outlet. The useful install is the one that fits the panel, the drive, and the vehicle you actually own."
	crumb="EV Charger Installation"
	serviceName="Level 2 EV charger installation"
	serviceType="EV charger installation"
	faqs={faqs.ev}
	ctaLabel="Plan Your EV Charger"
	ctaTitle="Plan your EV charger installation."
	ctaText="The vehicle, the panel, and where the charger should live are enough to start. A speed is quoted only after those are known."
	related={[
		{ href: '/unifi-networking/', label: 'UniFi networking', text: 'Connected chargers are happier on a network that reaches the garage.' },
		{ href: '/wireless-bridges/', label: 'Wireless bridges', text: 'When the garage is a separate building and still needs a signal.' },
		{ href: '/contact/', label: 'Discuss the panel', text: 'Photos of the panel and the parking spot save a round of guessing.' },
	]}
>
	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="problem-heading">A wall outlet is a slow compromise.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Charging overnight from a standard 120-volt receptacle can work for very light driving. It is a poor fit
					for a commute, a cold week, or a second vehicle. Level 2 equipment, on a dedicated circuit, is the
					ordinary way to start the day with a useful charge.
				</p>
				<p>
					How many miles that returns depends on the vehicle, the charger, the circuit, and the electrical system.
					Those numbers are established for your project. They are not borrowed from a brochure.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2 id="scope-heading">Residential, and light commercial where it fits.</h2>
			<ul class="index-list">
				<li>
					<strong>Installation</strong>
					<p>Level 2 chargers, hardwired units, and NEMA receptacles where a receptacle is the right choice.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The circuit</strong>
					<p>Dedicated EV circuits, breaker installation, and conductor sizing for a continuous load.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The building</strong>
					<p>Garage upgrades, outdoor placements, and the distance from the panel to the parking spot.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Connected chargers</strong>
					<p>WiFi or network connectivity when the charger uses it for scheduling, load management, or status.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What is already there</strong>
					<p>Troubleshooting an existing EV circuit, replacing a charger, and planning for a second vehicle.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="panel-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="panel-heading">Is your electrical panel ready for an EV charger?</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					EV charging is a significant electrical load, and it runs for hours. That is different from a tool that
					draws power for a few minutes. The evaluation looks at the service capacity, the main panel rating, the
					loads already in the house, open breaker space, the charger’s amperage, continuous-load rules, conductor
					requirements, and how far the charger sits from the panel.
				</p>
				<p>
					Some properties have adequate capacity. Others need an electrical upgrade, load management, or a charging
					configuration that shares capacity instead of assuming the panel can take a second full circuit. A smart
					panel or a load-management device can, in the right calculation, avoid a service change. It does not
					replace that calculation, and it is not the right answer for every house.
				</p>
				<p>Future vehicles are cheaper to plan for now than to discover after the first conduit is closed up.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="code-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="code-heading">Code, permits, and the manufacturer’s instructions.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					All electrical work is performed in accordance with applicable electrical codes, local permitting
					requirements, manufacturer instructions, and licensing requirements, including inspection where it applies.
				</p>
				{
					site.licenses.length > 0 ? (
						<ul>
							{site.licenses.map((license) => (
								<li>
									{license.label}: {license.value}
								</li>
							))}
						</ul>
					) : null
				}
				<p>
					A consultation does not itself pull a permit or create a construction contract. Scope, price, and the
					path through the local authority are set in the project agreement.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ServiceLayout>
